Polycentric Constitutionalism v0.6

A Testable Architecture of Diversity-Preserving Coordination and Adaptive Institutional Resilience

---

Abstract

This paper advances a formalized theoretical framework—polycentric constitutionalism—for the simultaneous preservation of political, cultural, and intellectual diversity and the improvement of collective coordination, adaptive stability, and peaceful coexistence in complex plural societies. The framework does not claim to originate the convergence of polycentricity and modus vivendi, established by Aligica and Murtazashvili (2025) [L], nor to be the first to integrate interoperability and contestability into a polycentric constitutional framework, anticipated by Aligica, Manor, and Tarko (2026) [L] and Sahely (2026) [L]. Version 0.6 prunes the hypothesis set to four core falsifiable claims (H6–H9), demotes the structural macro-historical propositions (H1–H5) to background conditions, replaces three separate scope thresholds with a latent state-capacity factor constructed by principal component analysis, adopts a profile-dominance scoring rule for contestability, replaces quadratic OLS with generalized additive models for non-linear revision-cost effects, and specifies a placebo test for the epistemic-interoperability hypothesis. The measurement protocol enforces source separation between mechanisms and outcomes, raises the validation threshold to Krippendorff's α ≥ 0.667, and treats the three domination-success cases (Singapore, post-1994 Rwanda, COVID-19 emergency governance) as scored configurations rather than narrative illustrations. The novelty claim remains one of formal precision, operational discipline, and falsifiable design, not conceptual discovery.

Keywords: polycentricity; modus vivendi; non-domination; epistemic interoperability; differentiated reversibility; conditional contestability; Pareto analysis; minimum viable measurement; domination-success; placebo test

---

1. Introduction

1.1 The Problem

Complex societies must coordinate collective action across jurisdictional, cultural, and epistemic divides without purchasing coordination through homogenization. This is the coordination–domination dilemma: coordination requires centralization, but centralization enables domination. Sahely (2026) names this problem explicitly and develops a middle-range institutional framework for investigating it [L]. This paper shares that diagnosis but specifies a different analytical apparatus, organized around a small set of falsifiable conditional hypotheses and accompanied by a measurement protocol that enforces independence between mechanism indicators and outcome indicators.

1.2 What Is Already Established

The convergence thesis (Aligica & Murtazashvili, 2025) argues for polycentricity and modus vivendi as a joint framework for tolerant society [L]. The interoperability thesis (Aligica, Manor & Tarko, 2026) argues that interoperability offers a more flexible and practical approach to managing heterogeneous governance landscapes than consensus, prioritizing functional compatibility [L]. Polycentric constitutional ecology (Sahely, 2026) develops a framework of differentiated and partially autonomous institutions structured through rights, competencies, obligations, information pathways, coordination, accountability, and review, explicitly addressing the coordination–domination dilemma [L]. Pettit (1999) establishes that non-domination requires institutionalized contestability [L]. The 2026 collegial-AI preprint proposes structured collegia of AI models as a governance architecture [L].

1.3 What This Paper Claims

The contribution is restricted to the following specifications. Each is tagged [S] and each is falsifiable.

C1. Pareto formulation with treated-exogenous diversity floor. The normative target is the Pareto set of institutional configurations satisfying the diversity floor (Section 2.2). The floor D_min is treated as exogenous for the short-run empirical tests of this paper; its endogenization as a second-order constitutional bargain is reserved for future work.

C2. Five-component non-destructive interoperability protocol. The mapping Φ is specified as a tuple preserving translated representation, provenance, evidence characterization, uncertainty, and validity conditions (Section 3.1).

C3. Revision-cost model with GAM estimation. The relationship between revision costs and adaptive stability is left non-parametric and estimated with generalized additive models rather than quadratic OLS (Section 3.2).

C4. Profile-dominance contestability scoring. The multiplicative aggregate is retained only as a robustness check; the primary scoring rule is a profile-dominance rule that assigns 0 if any component is 0, and 2 only if every component is at least 1 and the mean is at least 1.5 (Section 3.3).

C5. Three-rule falsification protocol. Refutation, scope-limitation, and indeterminacy are distinguished independently of observed results (Section 6).

C6. Scored domination-success protocol. Singapore, post-1994 Rwanda, and COVID-19 emergency governance are coded across all measurement variables, not narrated (Section 7.2).

C7. Source-separated minimum viable measurement. Mechanism indicators are coded from procedural datasets, not from outcome indices (Section 8.2). A pre-registered validation requirement blocks hypothesis testing until Krippendorff's α ≥ 0.667 is achieved against a full-resolution coding of at least ten cases (Section 8.3).

C8. Placebo test for H7. Effective interoperability should not moderate the effect of political diversity on coordination. The placebo interaction provides a specification check against the possibility that the interoperability indicator proxies general state capacity (Section 9.4).

C9. Latent state-capacity factor for scope. Three separate thresholds are replaced by a single latent factor constructed by principal component analysis over Polity5 durability, V-Dem rule of law, and WGI government effectiveness, with out-of-scope defined as the bottom decile (Section 10.1).

C10. Identification devices for ℐ_eff. Exogenous procedural mandates, regulatory discontinuities (including the CSRD employment threshold subject to verification against the 2025 Omnibus revision), and domain-specific panel settings are specified as constructions to be developed, not as validated instruments (Section 9).

1.4 Traceability Convention

Propositions are tagged [L] when established in the literature, [I] when inferred from established mechanisms, [S] when specified by this paper (the restricted novelty claim), and [V] when requiring empirical or formal verification.

