2. Normative Structure

2.1 Core Definitions

Let D = (D_p, D_c, D_i) denote political, cultural, and intellectual diversity. Let K denote coordination capacity, S adaptive stability, and P peaceful coexistence. Let x ∈ 𝒳 denote an institutional configuration. Cultural diversity D_c is decomposed into two sub-dimensions that the Singapore case requires: D_c^seg, the autonomy to self-segregate, and D_c^rep, the capacity for cultural reproduction. The decomposition is [S].

2.2 The Pareto Formulation

The normative target is the set of non-dominated configurations satisfying the diversity floor:

\mathcal{P} = \left\{ x \in \mathcal{F} : \nexists y \in \mathcal{F} \text{ such that } K(y) \geq K(x), \, S(y) \geq S(x), \, P(y) \geq P(x), \text{ with at least one strict inequality} \right\}

where

\mathcal{F} = \{ x \in \mathcal{X} : D_j(x) \geq D_{j,\min} \ \forall j \}

2.3 Status of the Diversity Floor

The floor D_min is a constitutional choice, not an output of optimization. For the short-run empirical tests specified in Sections 4 and 7, D_min is treated as exogenous. Its endogenization as a second-order bargain under a veil of uncertainty, following the transaction-cost logic of constitutional amendment (Buchanan & Tullock, 1962), is reserved for future work and is not claimed as a result of this paper. The consequence is acknowledged: hypotheses H6–H9 are conditional on a society having already adopted a diversity floor, and the framework does not explain why a society would adopt one.

---

3. Three Mechanisms and the Transversal Condition

The architecture comprises three mechanisms and one transversal condition. Metagovernance ℳ is the coordination mechanism; epistemic interoperability ℐ is the cognitive mechanism; differentiated reversibility ℛ is the temporal mechanism; effective contestability 𝒞 is the transversal legitimacy condition applying to all authority.

3.1 Epistemic Interoperability

Epistemic interoperability is the institutional capacity to translate claims generated within one knowledge tradition into a format that is comparable, contestable, and accountable within another tradition, without requiring convergence on methods or first principles. The five-component non-destructive protocol is:

\Phi(\mathcal{K}_j) = \langle T_j, P_j, E_j, U_j, V_j \rangle

where T_j is the translated representation, P_j provenance, E_j evidence characterization, U_j uncertainty, and V_j validity conditions. The 2026 APEX Epistemic Interoperability Contract—a technical specification hosted on GitHub, not an academic publication—converges on the same functional requirements (intent bound, evidence typed, uncertainty preserved, authority valid, contradictions exposed), but this convergence does not constitute academic corroboration and must be cited as a parallel technical construction.

The hypothesis, stated at the theoretical and empirical levels:

\frac{\partial^2 K}{\partial D_i \, \partial I_{\text{eff}}} > 0 \quad \text{(H7)}

K_{it} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 D_{i,it} + \beta_2 I_{\text{eff},it} + \beta_3 (D_{i,it} \times I_{\text{eff},it}) + \gamma Z_{it} + \varepsilon_{it}

with H7 asserting \beta_3 > 0. The hypothesis is probabilistic and conditional, not a biconditional. It does not claim that intellectual diversity cannot contribute without formal interoperability, nor that formal interoperability guarantees a positive contribution.

3.2 Differentiated Reversibility

The paper replaces revision rates with institutional revision costs:

c_{\text{policy}} < c_{\text{competence}} < c_{\text{rights}}

The three-tier structure assigns ordinary revision to policies, reinforced revision to competences, and protection of the core against ordinary majorities with reinforced revision of the periphery to rights. Costs include legal, democratic, procedural, and domination-risk components. The relationship between revision costs and adaptive stability is left non-parametric and estimated with generalized additive models:

S = \alpha + f_1(c_{L_1}) + f_2(c_{L_2}) + f_3(c_{L_3}) + \gamma \mathcal{C} + \delta Z + \varepsilon

where f_k are smooth functions estimated from the data. The inverted-U expectation for c_{L_1} is stated as an exploratory hypothesis, not as an imposed functional form. The quadratic OLS specification of v0.5 is abandoned because a quadratic over three ordinal values is unidentified. Continuous measurement of c_{L_3} uses the Lutz amendment-difficulty index; continuous measurement of c_{L_1} uses time-to-amendment and veto-player counts.

3.3 Conditional Contestability

The five-component profile is:

\mathcal{C}(A) = \langle A_c, I_n, E_f, R_m, P_r \rangle

with components for accessibility, independence of the adjudicating body, effectiveness of the remedy, remedy capacity, and protection against retaliation. The multiplicative aggregate A_c \cdot I_n \cdot E_f \cdot R_m \cdot P_r is retained only as a robustness check. The primary scoring rule is profile dominance, specified in Section 8.1. The index is not interpreted as an objective universal measure of non-domination; it is a framework-proposed instrument requiring validation, and it is coded from procedural datasets rather than outcome indices (Section 8.2).

The four cases retained are: central intervention to protect fundamental rights with high effective contestability (legitimate); centralization without effective contestability (domination); community practice harming members without exit or voice (intra-community domination); one authority immune to challenge by other authorities (inter-institutional domination).

3.4 Interaction Effects H8 and H9

H8: \partial^2 S / \partial D_p \, \partial \mathcal{R} > 0; empirically, \beta_3 > 0 in a model of S on D_p, \mathcal{R}_{\text{eff}}, and their interaction.

H9: \partial^2 P / \partial \mathcal{C} \, \partial \mathcal{M} > 0; empirically, \beta_3 > 0 in a model of P on \mathcal{C}, \mathcal{M}_{\text{eff}}, and their interaction.

Independence requirement: indices for ℳ, ℐ, ℛ, and 𝒞 must be constructed from structural and procedural data sources entirely separate from the outcome variables K, S, and P. A metagovernance index is not effective merely because conflict declined; that would confound mechanism with outcome.

---

4. Core Falsifiable Hypotheses

The falsification protocol of this paper is restricted to four hypotheses. H1–H5 are demoted to background conditions (Section 5).

H6 (Effective interoperability). Intellectual diversity improves collective problem-solving only when effective epistemic interoperability—not merely formal presence—is institutionalized; in its absence, intellectual diversity increases polarization and decision paralysis.

H7 (Interoperability amplifies diversity). \partial^2 K / \partial D_i \, \partial I_{\text{eff}} > 0; empirically, \beta_3 > 0 in the specification of Section 3.1.

H8 (Revision costs stabilize diversity). \partial^2 S / \partial D_p \, \partial \mathcal{R} > 0; empirically, \beta_3 > 0 in the specification of Section 3.4.

H9 (Contestability and metagovernance sustain peace). \partial^2 P / \partial \mathcal{C} \, \partial \mathcal{M} > 0; empirically, \beta_3 > 0 in the specification of Section 3.4.

Each hypothesis is a probabilistic conditional claim about a cross-partial derivative. Each is testable in bounded domains with observable mechanism and outcome indicators. H6–H9 are the only claims of this paper that are falsification targets.

---

5. Background Conditions (H1–H5 Demoted)

The following propositions are treated as background conditions supported by the existing literature and by synthetic-control and comparative-historical evidence. They are not falsification targets of this paper. They specify the structural context within which H6–H9 are evaluated.

B1 (Protected multiplicity). Constitutionally protected multiple jurisdictions preserve diversity at higher levels than systems where diversity depends on central toleration, controlling for historical and economic variables. Established in the polycentric governance literature [L]; treated here as a background condition.

B2 (Metagovernance). Cross-jurisdictional information-sharing mechanisms and enforceable coordination obligations are associated with lower rates of unresolved interjurisdictional conflict, controlling for administrative capacity and power asymmetry. Established in the metagovernance literature [L]; background condition.

B3 (Differentiated revision costs). Systems with differentiated revision costs exhibit higher adaptive stability than systems with uniform high or uniform low costs. The structural claim is supported by the constitutional-amendment literature [L]; the parametric form is left to the GAM specification of Section 3.2 [S].

B4 (Two-tier settlement). Constitutional orders combining a thin meta-consensus with modus vivendi exhibit lower rates of multidimensional constitutional degradation than orders founded on comprehensive consensus or pure modus vivendi. Established in the modus vivendi literature [L]; background condition.

B5 (Complementarity). Under high polarization, consociational guarantees and agonistic arenas are conditionally complementary: systems possessing both outperform systems possessing only one on peace and adaptability. Consociational guarantees are not claimed to be universally necessary [L]; background condition.

Demoting B1–B5 has a methodological cost and a methodological benefit. The cost is that the framework no longer claims to test the structural conditions that make diversity-preserving coordination possible. The benefit is that the four remaining hypotheses are cleaner, easier to falsify, and not entangled with macro-historical dynamics that require decades of observation to resolve.

---

6. Falsification Protocol

Rule A (Refutation). If the mechanisms are defined and measured independently, if scope conditions are satisfied, and if the expected interactions are persistently null or negative, the corresponding hypothesis fails.

Rule B (Scope limitation). If a negative result is explained by a pre-specified scope condition (Section 10), the result limits the domain of application without constituting a general refutation.

Rule C (Indeterminacy). If indicators are insufficient or observations contradictory, the result is indeterminate. It must not be transformed into confirmation.

Success criterion. An improvement in K is not sufficient. The framework must verify that it is not accompanied by a reduction in D or in 𝒞.

Domination-success criterion. A case constitutes a domination-success if

\Delta K > 0, \quad \Delta D < 0 \ \text{or} \ \Delta \mathcal{C} < 0

This is not a success of the normative model, even if the coordination indicator increases.

---

7. Comparative Protocol

7.1 Three Categories

The protocol includes three categories of cases. Coordination under diversity: Switzerland, India. Diversity without coordination: Belgium, Bosnia and Herzegovina. Domination-success: Singapore, Rwanda, COVID-19 emergency governance.

The Belgium case plays a specific role beyond illustration. Belgium possesses formal metagovernance mechanisms (federal coordination bodies, linguistic parity rules) yet exhibits persistent decision blockage. Under the framework, this is not a refutation of H9 but a prediction: formal metagovernance without effective contestability and without effective epistemic interoperability should produce coordination failure. Belgium is therefore a specification check on the independence-enforcement rule of Section 3.4.

7.2 Scored Domination-Success Cases

The three cases are coded on the measurement variables of Section 8, with source separation enforced. The scores below are provisional and require validation under Section 8.3 before use in hypothesis testing.

Singapore (1965–present). The Ethnic Integration Policy, introduced in 1989, imposes ethnic quotas in HDB public housing. The initial quotas were 22 percent Malay, 84 percent Chinese, and 10 percent Indian/Other at neighbourhood level, and 25 percent Malay, 87 percent Chinese, and 13 percent Indian at block level. The policy was designed to prevent any group from dominating a block or neighbourhood. Provisional scores: D_p = 0, D_c^seg = 0, D_c^rep = 1, 𝒞 = 0, K = 2, S = 2. The case exhibits ΔK > 0, ΔD_c^seg < 0, and Δ𝒞 < 0. The decomposition of D_c is required because the government presents the EIP as preserving cultural reproduction while constraining residential self-segregation; the framework must code both dimensions separately to avoid conflating them.

Rwanda (post-1994). The Rwandan parliament dissolved the Mouvement démocratique républicain (MDR) on 15 April 2003 following a commission report accusing it of propagating divisionist ideology. The MDR was one of eight parties in the national unity government. Provisional scores: D_p = 0, D_c = 1, 𝒞 = 0, K = 2, S = 2. The case exhibits ΔK > 0, ΔD_p < 0, and Δ𝒞 < 0. The WGI government effectiveness score for Rwanda in the 2000s was approximately −0.9, which under a threshold rule would place the case out of scope. This is the reason Section 10 replaces separate thresholds with a latent state-capacity factor: a single threshold risks excluding precisely the cases the framework needs to examine.

COVID-19 emergency governance (2020–2022). The OECD documents that numerous states adopted emergency laws centralizing power at the pandemic's outset, with central governments taking control of subnational responsibilities; Switzerland temporarily recentralized health management. Provisional scores: D_p = 1 (temporary), 𝒞 = 1 (de jure suspension, de facto use partial), K = 2 (short term), S = 1 (mixed), P = 2. The case exhibits ΔK > 0 and Δ𝒞 < 0 in the short term. The coding distinguishes de jure suspension from de facto use: many emergency laws were never fully applied. The case tests whether emergency centralization is reversible and whether the contestability loss is temporary or permanent.

The scores are provisional because the MVM validation requirement of Section 8.3 has not yet been satisfied. Until it is, the cases function as candidate configurations for screening, not as confirmatory evidence.

