8. Measurement Protocol

8.1 Profile-Dominance Scoring

The multiplicative aggregate is replaced as the primary scoring rule by a profile-dominance rule over the five contestability components:

\mathcal{C}_{\text{score}} =
\begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } \min(A_c, I_n, E_f, R_m, P_r) = 0 \\
2 & \text{if } \min(A_c, I_n, E_f, R_m, P_r) \geq 1 \ \text{and mean} \geq 1.5 \\
1 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

The same profile-dominance rule applies to the mechanism variables ℳ, ℐ_eff, and ℛ. The multiplicative aggregate is retained only as a robustness check and is reported alongside the profile score in all empirical applications. The profile-dominance rule preserves the theoretical property that a single catastrophic failure renders the mechanism non-functional, while avoiding the zero-inflation problem that makes the multiplicative aggregate unusable for ordinal scoring across large panels.

8.2 Source Separation

Mechanism indicators are coded from procedural datasets. Contestability is coded from constitutional texts, judicial appointment rules, and procedural codes, not from V-Dem access-to-justice indices. Metagovernance is coded from constitutional and statutory provisions, not from conflict-resolution outcomes. Epistemic interoperability is coded from translation mandates and reporting standards, not from policy adoption rates. Reversibility is coded from amendment procedures and the Lutz index, not from observed amendment frequency. Outcome indicators K, S, and P may use V-Dem, WGI, UCDP, and Polity5. The separation is enforced at the coding stage, not only at the analysis stage: a mechanism indicator and an outcome indicator may not share a source dataset.

The rationale is that V-Dem's high-level indices are constructed from overlapping sub-indicators, and using them for both mechanism and outcome would produce correlations by construction. The concern is not merely theoretical; it is the practical reason the independence requirement of Section 3.4 cannot be satisfied if both sides of the regression draw from the same index family.

8.3 Validation Requirement

Before any test of H6–H9, the MVM protocol must be validated against a full-resolution coding of at least ten cases by two independent coders. The validation criterion is Krippendorff's α ≥ 0.667, which Krippendorff (2004) treats as the threshold for provisional conclusions. The v0.5 threshold of 0.6 is abandoned because it is below Krippendorff's own recommendation. If α falls below 0.667, the MVM cannot be used for hypothesis testing and the framework must rely on full-resolution coding for the restricted case set.

The validation requirement is a blocking condition. No test of H6–H9 is reported in this paper. The paper specifies what would count as a test and what would count as a validation of the instrument, and it states that the instrument is not yet validated.

8.4 Screening and Testing

The MVM profile score serves as a screening instrument. Cases flagged as outliers on the profile score—either as candidate coordination successes under diversity or as candidate domination-successes—trigger full-resolution coding before hypothesis testing. The MVM is not a substitute for full-resolution coding; it is a filter that reduces the cost of full-resolution coding across large panels.

