9. Identification Strategy

9.1 Exogenous Procedural Mandates

Legal or treaty obligations requiring standardized cross-jurisdictional reporting, translation, or data-sharing can serve as instruments for ℐ_eff when they are imposed by an external authority and are not adopted in anticipation of coordination outcomes. The exclusion restriction requires that the mandate affects K only through its effect on ℐ_eff. This device is a construction to be developed case by case; no validated instrument currently exists.

9.2 Regulatory Discontinuities

Threshold-based obligations create quasi-experimental variation. The Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive applies to firms exceeding two of three criteria: more than 250 employees, a balance sheet above 25 million euros, or net turnover above 50 million euros. The 2025 Omnibus Package proposes raising these thresholds to 1,000 employees and 450 million euros; the discontinuity at 250 employees may therefore be modified, and any regression-discontinuity design must be verified against the version applicable at the time of the study. The Open Data Directive provides a second candidate. The use of these discontinuities to identify ℐ_eff is a construction, not a validated design.

9.3 Domain Panels

Testing H6 and H7 in bounded regulatory domains—cross-border water management, inter-agency public health response, multi-jurisdictional AI safety audits—allows direct observation of input intellectual diversity and translation protocols before outcome coordination is measured. The temporal ordering within the domain addresses reverse causality.

9.4 Placebo Test for H7

If ℐ_eff truly moderates the effect of intellectual diversity on coordination, it should not moderate the effect of political diversity on coordination in the same domain. The placebo specification is:

K_{it} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 D_{p,it} + \beta_2 I_{\text{eff},it} + \beta_3 (D_{p,it} \times I_{\text{eff},it}) + \gamma Z_{it} + \varepsilon_{it}

with the expectation \beta_3 \approx 0. A non-zero placebo interaction indicates that the ℐ_eff indicator proxies general state capacity rather than epistemic interoperability specifically. The placebo test is an innovation of this paper, not a standard practice in the literature; it is presented as a specification check that the framework proposes and that future work must evaluate.

9.5 Causal Discipline

The framework begins with structured comparative analysis of bounded periods and domains using process tracing and longitudinal comparison. Global statistical modelling is not attempted before the MVM validation requirement is satisfied and before at least one domain panel has been coded at full resolution. Causal claims are conditional on the identification strategy and are reported with explicit limitations.

---

10. Scope Conditions

10.1 Latent State-Capacity Factor

Three separate thresholds are replaced by a single latent factor constructed by principal component analysis over Polity5 regime durability, V-Dem rule of law, and WGI government effectiveness. The first principal component is retained as the state-capacity factor. Out-of-scope is defined as the bottom decile of the factor distribution at t_0. The bottom-decile cutoff is an authorial specification, subject to sensitivity analysis.

The replacement addresses a specific problem. Under the v0.5 thresholds, Rwanda post-1994 would be out of scope (WGI approximately −0.9), which would exclude a case the framework needs to examine. A single latent factor avoids the arbitrary exclusion produced by any one threshold while retaining the theoretical function of scope conditions: to distinguish scope limitation (Rule B) from theoretical failure (Rule A).

10.2 Conflict Threshold

The UCDP definition of armed conflict is an incompatibility contested by both parties resulting in at least 25 battle-related deaths in a calendar year. The absolute threshold is canonical. A normalized measure of battle-related deaths per 100,000 population is reported as a sensitivity check, not as a component of the canonical threshold; the normalization is a derived practice in quantitative epidemiology and is not part of the UCDP codebook.

10.3 Sensitivity and Confounding

For each scope threshold and for the latent factor cutoff, results are reported under alternative cutoffs of plus or minus ten percent. An E-value is reported for each estimated interaction to quantify the minimum strength of unobserved confounding that would explain away the result. The E-value is reported even when the result is null, because a null result with a low E-value is less informative than a null result with a high E-value.

---

11. Extension: AI-Assisted Epistemic Coordination

This section is an experimental research agenda, not a constitutive part of the constitutional theory. Its falsification conditions are distinct.

Multiple AI systems may share training data, documentary sources, representational biases, and optimization objectives; apparent diversity may mask deep correlation of errors. A four-layer architecture is specified: human constitutional authority, independent AI systems, human experts, and an evidence-and-disagreement interface carrying provenance, uncertainty, contradictions, and audit. A collegium whose errors are highly correlated does not provide genuine epistemic redundancy; the relevant condition is independence of errors, measured on common tasks with external evaluation criteria. Three systems repeating the same error do not constitute three independent proofs; collective decisions require human deliberation, not model voting.

Recent work on verification panels indicates that the sign of the diversity effect depends on the correlation of errors—diversity harms under correlated errors and helps under independent errors. This work is at preprint stage and is cited as convergence, not as an established result. The exploratory hypothesis is

\frac{\partial K}{\partial D_{\text{AI}}} = f(I_{\text{AI}}, \mathcal{C}_{\text{AI}}, Q_{\text{AI}}, Z)

where Q_AI measures error independence. This is a conjecture, not a result.

---

12. Conclusion

The contribution of polycentric constitutionalism v0.6 is restricted to ten specifications, each tagged [S] in Section 1.3. The paper does not claim to originate the polycentricity–modus vivendi convergence, to be the first to integrate interoperability and contestability into a polycentric constitutional framework, or to invent tiered amendment or Pettit's contestability criterion. It claims to provide a falsifiable formalization of four conditional hypotheses, a source-separated measurement protocol with an explicit validation requirement, a scored domination-success protocol that avoids the exclusion of the cases the framework most needs to examine, and a placebo test that specifies a condition under which the epistemic-interoperability hypothesis should be rejected.

The framework applies to societies whose state-capacity factor lies above the bottom decile at t_0. Where this condition is violated, predictions are indeterminate, and the result is classified as scope limitation rather than theoretical failure.

