Appendix A: Pareto Set — Existence, Structure, and Comparative Statics

A.1 Purpose

This appendix establishes the formal properties of the normative target in Section 2.2. The claims are: (i) the feasible set is non-empty under stated conditions; (ii) the Pareto set is non-empty under stated conditions; (iii) the Pareto set is not generally a singleton; (iv) comparative statics on D_min are well-defined.

A.2 Formal Setting

Let \mathcal{X} be the space of institutional configurations, endowed with a topology. Let K, S, P: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R} be the three outcome functions. Let D: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}^3 with D = (D_p, D_c, D_i). Let D_{\min} \in \mathbb{R}^3 be the constitutional floor. Define

\mathcal{F} = \{ x \in \mathcal{X} : D_j(x) \geq D_{j,\min} \ \forall j \in \{p, c, i\} \}

and

\mathcal{P} = \left\{ x \in \mathcal{F} : \nexists y \in \mathcal{F} \text{ with } (K(y), S(y), P(y)) \geq (K(x), S(x), P(x)) \text{ and at least one strict} \right\}

where the inequality is the componentwise order on \mathbb{R}^3.

A.3 Assumption Set

A1 (Compactness). \mathcal{F} is non-empty and compact in the topology on \mathcal{X}.

A2 (Continuity). K, S, P are continuous on \mathcal{F}.

A3 (Boundedness). K, S, P are bounded on \mathcal{F}.

These assumptions are substantive. Compactness of \mathcal{F} requires that the diversity floor is not so high that no configuration satisfies it, and that the configuration space is not unbounded in a way that permits escape to infinity. Continuity is a regularity condition on the outcome functions. Boundedness is a normalization.

A.4 Proposition 1 (Non-emptiness of the Feasible Set)

Under A1, \mathcal{F} \neq \emptyset.

Proof. Immediate from A1. \square

The proposition is trivial but methodologically important: the framework presupposes that the diversity floor is feasible. If empirical work finds that no configuration in the observed set satisfies D_min, the appropriate response is not to lower the floor but to recognize that the observed set is outside the scope of the framework.

A.5 Proposition 2 (Non-emptiness of the Pareto Set)

Under A1–A3, \mathcal{P} \neq \emptyset.

Proof. The image of \mathcal{F} under the map x \mapsto (K(x), S(x), P(x)) is a compact subset of \mathbb{R}^3 by continuity and compactness. Every non-empty compact subset of \mathbb{R}^n has at least one maximal element under the componentwise order (this is the finite-dimensional version of the Bourbaki–Witt theorem applied to the partial order, or equivalently a direct application of Zorn's lemma to the compact set). The preimage of any maximal element under the map is in \mathcal{P}. Therefore \mathcal{P} \neq \emptyset. \square

The proposition guarantees that the framework has a non-empty normative target. It does not guarantee uniqueness.

A.6 Proposition 3 (Non-uniqueness)

The Pareto set is not generally a singleton.

Proof by construction. Consider a two-dimensional example with K(x) = x, S(x) = 1 - x, P(x) = 0 constant, and \mathcal{F} = [0,1]. The Pareto set is the entire interval [0,1], since no point dominates another on both K and S. The example generalizes: whenever the outcome functions trade off against one another, the Pareto set has positive dimension. \square

The non-uniqueness is not a defect. It is the substantive content of the Pareto formulation: the framework identifies a set of admissible configurations, not a unique optimum. The choice among Pareto-efficient configurations is a political choice, not a mathematical one.

A.7 Proposition 4 (Comparative Statics on the Diversity Floor)

Let D_{\min} \leq D_{\min}' componentwise. Then \mathcal{F}' \subseteq \mathcal{F}, and consequently \mathcal{P}' \subseteq \mathcal{P} \cup \{x \in \mathcal{P} : x \notin \mathcal{F}'\}.

Proof. If D_{\min} \leq D_{\min}', then for any x with D_j(x) \geq D_{j,\min}' for all j, we also have D_j(x) \geq D_{j,\min} for all j. Hence \mathcal{F}' \subseteq \mathcal{F}. The Pareto set of a subset is contained in the Pareto set of the superset union the removed points. \square

The proposition formalizes the intuition that raising the diversity floor shrinks the feasible set and can only shrink or shift the Pareto set, never expand it. This is the formal content of the claim in Section 2.3 that D_min is a constitutional choice with normative consequences, not an output of optimization.

A.8 Operational Consequence

The framework does not attempt to derive D_min from the outcome functions. It treats D_min as a parameter and reports results conditional on its value. This is the sense in which the framework is a theory of conditional diversity-preserving coordination: it does not claim that a society ought to adopt any particular floor, only that if a floor is adopted, the Pareto structure of the feasible set has the properties established above.

