Appendix B: Non-Destructive Interoperability — Formal Properties

B.1 Purpose

This appendix formalizes the five-component protocol of Section 3.1 and establishes the non-destructive property.

B.2 Formal Setting

Let \mathcal{K}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{K}_n be knowledge traditions. Each tradition \mathcal{K}_j is a set of claims, each with an evidence base E_j, inference rules R_j, and a set of validity conditions V_j. A claim c \in \mathcal{K}_j carries a provenance P(c) \subseteq \mathcal{K}_j (the sub-tradition from which it derives) and an uncertainty U(c) \in [0,1].

The interoperability map is

\Phi: \bigcup_{j=1}^n \mathcal{K}_j \to \mathcal{C}^5

where \mathcal{C}^5 is the five-component space of translated representations:

\Phi(c) = \langle T(c), P(c), E(c), U(c), V(c) \rangle

with T(c) the translated representation, P(c) provenance, E(c) evidence characterization, U(c) uncertainty, and V(c) validity conditions.

B.3 Definition (Non-Destructiveness)

A translation \Phi is non-destructive if there exists a reconstruction map

\Psi: \mathcal{C}^5 \to \bigcup_{j=1}^n \mathcal{K}_j

such that for every c \in \mathcal{K}_j, the reconstructed claim \Psi(\Phi(c)) is a member of the same tradition \mathcal{K}_j and preserves the provenance, evidence, uncertainty, and validity conditions of c.

The definition does not require that \Psi(\Phi(c)) = c. It requires that the reconstruction is recognizable as a claim of the same tradition and that the four components are preserved. This allows paraphrase and reformulation while prohibiting erasure.

B.4 Proposition 5 (Existence of Non-Destructive Translations)

A non-destructive translation exists for any pair of traditions \mathcal{K}_i, \mathcal{K}_j if and only if the two traditions share a common meta-language in which provenance, evidence, uncertainty, and validity can be expressed.

Proof (sketch). Sufficiency: if a common meta-language exists, define T(c) as the meta-linguistic representation of c, P(c) as the meta-linguistic description of provenance, and similarly for E, U, and V. The reconstruction map \Psi reads the meta-linguistic representation and re-expresses it in the vocabulary of \mathcal{K}_i. Necessity: if no common meta-language exists, no representation can simultaneously express the four components in a way recognizable to both traditions. \square

The proposition is the formal content of the claim in Section 3.1 that interoperability does not require convergence on methods. It requires a shared meta-language for describing methods, not agreement on which methods are correct. The meta-language is the interoperability infrastructure.

B.5 Proposition 6 (Non-Compositionality)

In general, \Phi(\mathcal{K}_i \cup \mathcal{K}_j) \neq \Phi(\mathcal{K}_i) \cup \Phi(\mathcal{K}_j).

Proof by construction. Consider two traditions that classify a phenomenon differently. Tradition A classifies it as an instance of type X; tradition B classifies it as an instance of type Y, where X and Y are incompatible in either tradition alone. The translated representation of the union must record the incompatibility, which is information not present in either translation alone. Hence the union of translations is a proper subset of the translation of the union. \square

Non-compositionality is a feature, not a defect. It reflects the fact that interoperability generates new information—namely, information about the relations between traditions—that is not present in either tradition alone. This is the formal basis for the claim in Section 3.4 that interoperability can amplify the coordination value of diversity: the amplification comes from the new information generated at the interface.

B.6 Operational Specification

The five components are coded as follows. Provenance is coded from citation and derivation records; a claim with no traceable provenance scores 0. Evidence characterization is coded from the type of evidence invoked; a claim whose evidence type is not specified scores 0. Uncertainty is coded from the claim's stated confidence; a claim with no stated confidence scores 0. Validity conditions are coded from the claim's stated scope; a claim with no stated scope scores 0. Translated representation is coded from the availability of the claim in a form usable by another tradition; a claim available only in its original vocabulary scores 0.

The profile-dominance rule of Section 8.1 applies: a translation scores 0 if any component is 0, and 2 only if every component is at least 1 and the mean is at least 1.5. The multiplicative aggregate is retained only as a robustness check.

