Appendix C: Profile-Dominance Scoring — Properties and Proofs

C.1 Purpose

This appendix establishes the properties of the profile-dominance rule of Section 8.1 and compares it to the multiplicative aggregate.

C.2 Formal Definitions

Let x = (x_1, \ldots, x_k) \in [0,1]^k be a vector of components. The multiplicative aggregate is

M(x) = \prod_{i=1}^k x_i

The profile-dominance score is

P(x) =
\begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } \min_i x_i = 0 \\
2 & \text{if } \min_i x_i \geq 1/k \ \text{and } \frac{1}{k}\sum_i x_i \geq 1.5/k \\
1 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

The threshold \min_i x_i \geq 1/k requires every component to be at least 1/k, and the threshold on the mean requires the mean to be at least 1.5/k. The thresholds are calibrated so that a vector with all components equal to 1/k scores 1, a vector with all components equal to 2/k scores 2, and a vector with any component equal to 0 scores 0.

C.3 Proposition 7 (Zero-Inflation Preservation)

For any x, M(x) = 0 if and only if P(x) = 0.

Proof. M(x) = 0 iff at least one x_i = 0 iff \min_i x_i = 0 iff P(x) = 0. \square

The proposition establishes that the profile-dominance rule preserves the theoretical property that a single catastrophic failure renders the mechanism non-functional.

C.4 Proposition 8 (Avoidance of Arbitrary Smallness)

For any x with \min_i x_i > 0, M(x) can be made arbitrarily small by taking k large or by taking one component close to 0, whereas P(x) takes only the values 0, 1, 2.

Proof. If x_i = \epsilon for some i and x_j = 1 for all j \neq i, then M(x) = \epsilon, which can be made arbitrarily small. P(x) is either 0 (if \epsilon = 0), 1 (if 0 < \epsilon < 1/k), or 2 (if \epsilon \geq 1/k and the mean condition holds). \square

The proposition is the formal content of the criticism in Section 8.1: the multiplicative aggregate produces arbitrarily small values that are unusable for ordinal scoring, whereas the profile-dominance rule produces a coarse ordinal score.

C.5 Proposition 9 (Monotonicity)

P(x) is weakly monotone in each component: if x_i \leq x_i' for all i, then P(x) \leq P(x').

Proof. If x \leq x' componentwise, then \min_i x_i \leq \min_i x_i' and \frac{1}{k}\sum_i x_i \leq \frac{1}{k}\sum_i x_i'. The conditions defining P(x) = 0, P(x) = 1, and P(x) = 2 are nested in the corresponding conditions for x'. \square

The monotonicity is weak: P(x) = P(x') is possible even when x < x' strictly. This is the cost of coarsening. The benefit is that the score is comparable across cases without the scale artifacts of the multiplicative aggregate.

C.6 Proposition 10 (Discontinuity)

P(x) is not continuous in x.

Proof. Consider x = (1/k, 1/k, \ldots, 1/k). Then P(x) = 1. Consider x' = (1/k - \epsilon, 1/k, \ldots, 1/k) for small \epsilon > 0. Then P(x') = 1. But consider x'' = (0, 1/k, \ldots, 1/k). Then P(x'') = 0. The jump from 1 to 0 occurs at x_1 = 0, which is a discontinuity. \square

The discontinuity is a feature, not a defect. The theoretical claim is that a component at 0 is categorically different from a component at \epsilon, not merely quantitatively smaller. The profile-dominance rule encodes this categorical distinction.

C.7 Operational Consequence

The profile-dominance rule is the primary scoring rule. The multiplicative aggregate is reported alongside it as a robustness check. The two scores are not required to agree; when they disagree, the disagreement is itself informative and should be reported.

