Appendix D: GAM Specification and Identification for Revision Costs

D.1 Purpose

This appendix specifies the generalized additive model of Section 3.2 and establishes its identifiability conditions.

D.2 Formal Specification

The model is

S_i = \alpha + f_1(c_{L_1,i}) + f_2(c_{L_2,i}) + f_3(c_{L_3,i}) + \gamma \mathcal{C}_i + \delta Z_i + \varepsilon_i

where f_k are smooth functions, \mathcal{C}_i is the contestability score, Z_i is a vector of controls, and \varepsilon_i is an error term. The smooth functions are represented in a spline basis:

f_k(c) = \sum_{j=1}^{J_k} \beta_{kj} b_{kj}(c)

where b_{kj} are basis functions (thin-plate splines or cubic regression splines) and \beta_{kj} are coefficients.

D.3 Identification Constraints

The basis representation is not identified without constraints. The standard constraints are:

D1 (Centering). \sum_{i=1}^n f_k(c_{k,i}) = 0 for each k. This absorbs the constant into \alpha.

D2 (Penalization). The coefficients \beta_{kj} are penalized by a quadratic penalty \lambda_k \beta_k^T S_k \beta_k, where S_k is a penalty matrix and \lambda_k is a smoothing parameter. The penalty resolves the trade-off between fit and smoothness and ensures that the model is identified in the presence of collinearity among basis functions.

D3 (Basis dimension). J_k is chosen to be small enough to avoid overfitting and large enough to capture non-linearity. A typical choice is J_k = 5 for a single covariate with moderate non-linearity.

Under D1–D3, the model is identified and the smooth functions are estimable.

D.4 Proposition 11 (Consistency of GAM Estimator)

Under standard regularity conditions (independence of errors, finite variance, correct specification of the smooth functions up to the basis dimension), the GAM estimator of f_k is consistent as n \to \infty.

Proof. The result is standard in the GAM literature (Hastie & Tibshirani, 1990; Wood, 2017). The proof proceeds by showing that the penalized likelihood is asymptotically equivalent to a projection onto the spline space, and that the projection converges to the true function under the stated conditions. \square

The proposition is a standard result, not an original contribution. It is included to establish that the GAM specification is not ad hoc but rests on established statistical theory.

D.5 Testing the Inverted-U Hypothesis

The inverted-U hypothesis for c_{L_1} is that f_1 increases and then decreases. Under the GAM specification, this hypothesis is tested by comparing the estimated smooth function to a constant and to a linear function:

H_0^{\text{linear}}: f_1(c) = \beta c

H_0^{\text{constant}}: f_1(c) = 0

The test statistic is the difference in deviance between the restricted and unrestricted models, which is asymptotically chi-squared under the null. The effective degrees of freedom (edf) of the smooth term provide a descriptive measure: edf near 1 indicates a linear relationship, edf substantially greater than 1 indicates non-linearity.

The inverted-U is supported if the estimated f_1 has a maximum in the interior of the observed range of c_{L_1}. The location of the maximum is reported with a confidence interval.

D.6 Continuous Measurement of c_{L_1} and c_{L_3}

The v0.5 quadratic specification failed because c_{L_1} was ordinal over three values, which is unidentified with a quadratic term. The v0.6 specification uses continuous measurements.

For c_{L_3}, the Lutz amendment-difficulty index provides a continuous score from 1 to 10, constructed from the discrete steps of the amendment procedure. The index is established in the constitutional-amendment literature and is used as the continuous proxy.

For c_{L_1}, the continuous proxy is the sum of (i) the time from proposal to adoption of a policy change in months and (ii) the number of veto players whose agreement is required. The sum is standardized to mean 0 and standard deviation 1 for the analysis.

For c_{L_2}, the continuous proxy is the supermajority threshold required for competence revision, rescaled to [0,1].

The continuous measurements make the GAM specification identified and the inverted-U hypothesis testable.

