Appendix E: Contestability Index — Decomposition and Sensitivity

E.1 Purpose

This appendix establishes the properties of the five-component contestability profile of Section 3.3 and derives the sensitivity of the multiplicative aggregate.

E.2 Log-Linear Decomposition

The multiplicative aggregate is

C = A_c \cdot I_n \cdot E_f \cdot R_m \cdot P_r

Taking logarithms:

\log C = \log A_c + \log I_n + \log E_f + \log R_m + \log P_r

The log-linear form is additive: the log of the aggregate is the sum of the logs of the components.

E.3 Proposition 12 (Unit Elasticity)

For each component x \in \{A_c, I_n, E_f, R_m, P_r\}, the elasticity of C with respect to x is 1:

\frac{\partial \log C}{\partial \log x} = 1

Proof. Direct from the log-linear decomposition. \square

The proposition implies that a 10 percent decrease in any one component produces a 10 percent decrease in the aggregate. This is the formal content of the claim that the multiplicative form is harsh: any deterioration in any component is transmitted proportionally to the aggregate.

E.4 Proposition 13 (Marginal Rate of Substitution)

The marginal rate of substitution between any two components x and y is

MRS_{xy} = \frac{y}{x}

Proof. Along an indifference curve C = \text{const}, d\log C = 0, so dx/x + dy/y = 0, giving dy/dx = -y/x. \square

The MRS is the ratio of the components, not a constant. This means that the aggregate treats a unit of accessibility as more valuable when accessibility is low, and less valuable when accessibility is high. The property is standard for Cobb–Douglas-style aggregates.

E.5 Proposition 14 (Sensitivity to a Single Component)

The elasticity of C with respect to a single component x is 1, but the absolute change in C from a change \Delta x is

\Delta C = \frac{C}{x} \Delta x

which is largest when x is small.

Proof. dC = (C/x) dx by the chain rule. \square

The proposition formalizes the intuition that a small absolute improvement in a weak component produces a larger absolute improvement in the aggregate than the same improvement in a strong component. This is a normative property: the aggregate prioritizes the weakest link.

E.6 Profile-Dominance Rule (Restated)

The profile-dominance rule is

\mathcal{C}_{\text{score}} =
\begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } \min(A_c, I_n, E_f, R_m, P_r) = 0 \\
2 & \text{if } \min(A_c, I_n, E_f, R_m, P_r) \geq 1/5 \ \text{and mean} \geq 1.5/5 \\
1 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

The thresholds are calibrated so that the score is comparable across the five components. The score is not a substitute for the profile; it is a coarse summary for screening.

E.7 Operational Consequence

The five components are reported separately in all empirical applications. The multiplicative aggregate is reported as a robustness check. The profile-dominance score is the primary scoring rule. The framework does not claim that any of the three is an objective measure of non-domination; all three are instruments proposed by the framework and requiring validation.

