Appendix F: Placebo Test — Formal Specification and Power

F.1 Purpose

This appendix formalizes the placebo test of Section 9.4 and derives its power.

F.2 Formal Specification

The placebo test estimates

K_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 D_{p,i} + \beta_2 I_{\text{eff},i} + \beta_3 (D_{p,i} \times I_{\text{eff},i}) + \gamma Z_i + \varepsilon_i

and tests H_0: \beta_3 = 0. The substantive hypothesis is that effective interoperability does not moderate the effect of political diversity on coordination. If \beta_3 is non-zero, the interoperability indicator is plausibly proxying general state capacity rather than epistemic interoperability specifically.

F.3 Proposition 15 (Placebo as Specification Check)

Under the null that I_{\text{eff}} affects K only through its interaction with D_i, the placebo coefficient \beta_3 is zero in the population.

Proof. Under the null, the data-generating process for K is

K = g(D_i, I_{\text{eff}}, D_i \times I_{\text{eff}}, D_p, Z) + \varepsilon

with no D_p \times I_{\text{eff}} term. The population coefficient on D_p \times I_{\text{eff}} in the placebo regression is zero. \square

The proposition is trivial but methodologically important: the placebo test is a test of the specification, not of the substantive hypothesis. A rejection of the placebo indicates that the specification is wrong, not that H7 is wrong.

F.4 Power Analysis

The power of the placebo test depends on the sample size, the variance of the interaction term, the variance of the error term, and the size of the placebo coefficient under the alternative. The standard formula for the minimum detectable effect in a regression with a single interaction is

\text{MDE} = (t_{1-\alpha/2} + t_{1-\beta}) \cdot \text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_3)

where t_{1-\alpha/2} and t_{1-\beta} are the critical values for the test and the power, and \text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_3) is the standard error of the interaction coefficient.

For a sample of 1000 observations, with the interaction term standardized to unit variance and the residual standard deviation of 1, the standard error is approximately 0.032. At \alpha = 0.05 and power = 0.80, the MDE is approximately 0.09. A placebo coefficient of 0.09 or larger would be detected with 80 percent probability.

The power calculation is illustrative. The actual power depends on the empirical variance of the variables, which is unknown before the data are collected.

F.5 Operational Consequence

The placebo test is reported alongside the substantive test of H7. A rejection of the placebo is reported as evidence that the specification is wrong, not as evidence that H7 is wrong. A failure to reject the placebo is reported as consistency of the specification with the data, not as confirmation of H7.

