Appendix G: Latent State-Capacity Factor — PCA Construction

G.1 Purpose

This appendix specifies the construction of the latent state-capacity factor of Section 10.1 and establishes its properties.

G.2 Formal Construction

Let X_{1i}, X_{2i}, X_{3i} be the standardized values of Polity5 regime durability, V-Dem rule of law, and WGI government effectiveness for country i. Standardization is to mean 0 and standard deviation 1.

The first principal component is the linear combination

F_i = w_1 X_{1i} + w_2 X_{2i} + w_3 X_{3i}

where w = (w_1, w_2, w_3) is the eigenvector of the correlation matrix of (X_1, X_2, X_3) associated with the largest eigenvalue. The eigenvector is normalized to unit length.

G.3 Proposition 16 (Variance Maximization)

The first principal component maximizes the variance of the linear combination w_1 X_1 + w_2 X_2 + w_3 X_3 subject to \|w\| = 1.

Proof. Standard result in linear algebra: the maximizer of w^T \Sigma w subject to w^T w = 1 is the eigenvector of \Sigma associated with the largest eigenvalue. \square

The proposition establishes that the first principal component captures the maximum shared variance among the three indicators.

G.4 Proposition 17 (Orthogonality of Components)

The second and third principal components are orthogonal to the first and to each other.

Proof. Standard result: eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix associated with distinct eigenvalues are orthogonal. \square

The proposition establishes that the first component does not overlap with the second and third components. The first component is the shared signal; the second and third are residual variation.

G.5 Choice of the First Component

The first component is retained if it explains at least 60 percent of the total variance of the three indicators. This is a standard rule of thumb in PCA-based composite construction. If the first component explains less than 60 percent, the three indicators are not sufficiently unidimensional to justify a single composite, and the analysis reverts to the three separate indicators with a sensitivity analysis across specifications.

G.6 Out-of-Scope Definition

Out-of-scope is defined as the bottom decile of the distribution of F_i across the sample at t_0. The bottom-decile cutoff is an authorial specification, subject to sensitivity analysis with alternative cutoffs (5th, 15th, 20th percentiles).

The rationale for the bottom decile is that the framework requires a minimum of state capacity to operate its mechanisms. Below the bottom decile, the mechanisms are not expected to function, and the framework's predictions are indeterminate. This is a scope condition, not a substantive finding.

G.7 Proposition 18 (Monotonicity of Scope)

If the bottom-decile cutoff is raised, the out-of-scope set weakly expands.

Proof. Direct from the definition of the bottom decile. \square

The proposition formalizes the intuition that a stricter scope condition excludes more cases. The choice of cutoff is a trade-off between excluding cases where the mechanisms do not function and excluding cases where they might.

