Appendix H: MVM Protocol — Coding Rules and Validation

H.1 Purpose

This appendix specifies the coding rules for the minimum viable measurement protocol of Section 8 and the validation requirement of Section 8.3.

H.2 Coding Rules for Each Variable

D_p (political diversity). Code 0 if the constitution establishes a unitary state with no constitutionally protected subnational legislatures. Code 1 if the constitution establishes a federal or decentralized structure with at least one subnational legislature, but the subnational legislatures lack constitutionally protected competences. Code 2 if the constitution establishes a federal structure with constitutionally protected competences for subnational legislatures, and at least one of the following: asymmetric federalism, consociational guarantees, or non-territorial autonomy. Source: Comparative Constitutions Project, constitutional text.

D_c^seg (autonomy to self-segregate). Code 0 if residential or associational segregation is legally prohibited or administratively prevented. Code 1 if segregation is permitted but not protected. Code 2 if segregation is constitutionally protected as a group right. Source: constitutional text, statutory law, administrative regulations.

D_c^rep (capacity for cultural reproduction). Code 0 if minority languages and religious practices are legally suppressed. Code 1 if they are permitted in private but not in public institutions. Code 2 if they are supported by public institutions (education, media, religious establishment). Source: V-Dem cultural diversity indices, supplemented by constitutional text.

D_i (intellectual diversity). Code 0 if universities and media are under direct state control. Code 1 if they are formally independent but subject to significant informal control or self-censorship. Code 2 if they are institutionally independent with constitutional or statutory protections. Source: V-Dem academic freedom index, RSF press freedom index, statutory law.

K (coordination capacity). Code 0 if cross-jurisdictional problems are unresolved over multiple years. Code 1 if they are resolved on an ad hoc or crisis-driven basis. Code 2 if they are resolved through institutionalized routines. Source: WGI government effectiveness, supplemented by policy diffusion datasets.

S (adaptive stability). Code 0 if the regime experiences frequent unmanaged crises or collapses. Code 1 if the regime survives shocks through rigid preservation. Code 2 if the regime corrects errors without breakdown. Source: Polity5, V-Dem regime durability, supplemented by case-specific process tracing.

P (peaceful coexistence). Code 0 if the country experiences active civil conflict or pervasive state repression. Code 1 if conflict is latent or periodic. Code 2 if conflict is processed through peaceful institutional channels. Source: UCDP/PRIO, Global Peace Index.

ℳ (metagovernance). Code 0 if no cross-jurisdictional coordination bodies exist. Code 1 if such bodies exist but are informal or non-binding. Code 2 if such bodies exist with binding authority and enforcement mechanisms. Source: constitutional text, statutory law, institutional process tracing.

ℐ_eff (effective epistemic interoperability). Code 0 if there are no institutionalized translation mechanisms between knowledge traditions. Code 1 if such mechanisms exist but are partial or ad hoc. Code 2 if such mechanisms are institutionalized and non-destructive (all five components of Φ are at least 1). Source: audit of cross-domain regulatory and scientific integration frameworks.

ℛ (differentiated reversibility). Code 0 if revision costs are undifferentiated (all rigid or all fluid). Code 1 if there is partial differentiation or an inverted hierarchy. Code 2 if there is a strict three-tier hierarchy with continuous measurement on the Lutz index for the rights tier. Source: constitutional amendment rules, judicial review precedent analysis, Lutz index.

𝒞 (contestability). Code using the profile-dominance rule of Appendix C. Each of the five components is coded 0, 1, or 2, and the profile score is computed by the rule. Source: constitutional text, judicial appointment rules, statutory law, procedural codes.

H.3 Source Separation Enforcement

The coding rules enforce source separation at the coding stage. Mechanism indicators (ℳ, ℐ_eff, ℛ, 𝒞) are coded from constitutional texts, statutory law, and procedural codes. Outcome indicators (K, S, P) are coded from V-Dem, WGI, UCDP, and Polity5. The two sets of sources do not overlap. The separation is verified by an audit of the coding sheets: no mechanism indicator may be coded from a source that is also used for an outcome indicator.

H.4 Validation Protocol

Before any test of H6–H9, the MVM protocol is validated against a full-resolution coding of at least ten cases by two independent coders. The full-resolution coding uses the complete specification of each variable, not the MVM proxy. The validation criterion is Krippendorff's α ≥ 0.667, computed separately for each variable.

H.5 Proposition 19 (Blocking Condition)

If the validation criterion is not met for any variable, the MVM proxy for that variable cannot be used in hypothesis testing, and the analysis must either use full-resolution coding for the restricted case set or exclude the variable from the analysis.

Proof. By definition of the blocking condition. \square

The proposition is a methodological rule, not a mathematical theorem. It is included to make explicit that the framework does not proceed to hypothesis testing until the measurement instrument is validated.

H.6 Operational Consequence

The validation protocol is pre-registered before the coding begins. The full-resolution coding of the ten validation cases is reported in the supplementary materials. The Krippendorff α for each variable is reported. If α ≥ 0.667 is achieved for all variables, the MVM proxies are used for screening; if not, the analysis uses full-resolution coding for the restricted case set.

